From u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com  Sat Oct 10 16:35:15 2020
From: u3fe90052fbf2f899 at groups.google.com (Terrence Jorgensen)
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2020 16:35:15 +0000
Subject: Re: Two questions relating to latent growth model.
Message-ID: <G6oJE7gRpR0.O_xI4Cy6AAAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <G6oJE7gRpR0.cxkG0yojAgAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <G6oJE7gRpR0.cxkG0yojAgAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/G6oJE7gRpR0/m/O_xI4Cy6AAAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> I would like to ask 1) if the two forms (the syntax marked in red) are both correct or not, 2) if so, what is the difference between them, 3) when to involve 1 (mean structure) into the syntax.

You want to estimate the means of the growth factors, unless you have some reason to fix the average initial-status or average rate-of-change to zero.  Regressing on the constant (intercept + slope ~ 1) frees their means in the syntax, or you could also simply use the lavaan() argument int.lv.free=TRUE to do so automatically (see the ?lavOptions help page), which is what the growth() wrapper does automatically.  That is probably why you have seen syntax with and without explicit latent means.

> I know how to fit a piecewise latent growth model of the same knot, but have no idea how to fit the model of different knots even after I have read this paper "https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/pdf/10.1177/0013164413496812 <https://www.google.com/url?q=https%3A%2F%2Fjournals.sagepub.com%2Fdoi%2Fpdf%2F10.1177%2F0013164413496812&sa=D&sntz=1&usg=AFQjCNEFt4v0Z1uWVHSSaZHRfRhg4OP5Ow>". Could you please give me any further suggestions or examples I could follow?

I am guessing that paper implemented it in Mplus, using the TSCORES option, which allows each subject to have different fixed loadings.  That is essentially how you allow for different knots per person, and it is also possible using "Definition variables" in OpenMx, but not possible in lavaan because everyone needs to have the same Lambda matrix.

Terrence D. Jorgensen
Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics
Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen

From u12fdd832cacb825e at groups.google.com  Sat Oct 10 16:52:15 2020
From: u12fdd832cacb825e at groups.google.com (Patrick [Malone Quantitative])
