> Could someone please help me to solve the problem of running SEM with endogenous categorical variables?

> ... I got a warning

Then the model ran.  Warnings indicate there might be a problem, not that there is one.  Although I wouldn't be surprised if your model were empirically underidentified, given your are almost certainly estimating as many parameters as there are people.  If your data were continuous, your syntax implies estimating 174 parameters:

lav_partable_npar(lavaanify(sem0))

Some of your outcomes are ordinal, and depending on the number of categories, you might be estimating even more parameters.  It will tell you near the top of the summary() output how many.  You could consider modeling one outcome at a time so that N > nParams in each model, but the N / nParams ratio would still be quite small, so warnings like this would not be a surprise.  I would recommend the regsem package <https://cran.r-project.org/package=regsem>, but it does not accommodate ordinal outcomes.

FYI:

> fit0 <- sem(sem0, data=yfdata0, ordered=c("family","health","independence","objective","happiness","happiness"))

You listed "happiness" twice.

Terrence D. Jorgensen
Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics
Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen

